     On come now, just what character to you think putty would transmit
across the network when you type something like <shift>+<keypad 4>?
Assuming a typical PC-style keyboard, it's either going to be "4" or
the escape sequence issued (by the type of terminal you're choosing to
emulate) for the left arrow.  Neither of those choices is going to make
nethack think you meant shift+H to run to the left.

     Various ports of nethack are able to do interesting things with
number pad keys because they are accessing the actual keypress info
rather than receiving characters that such keypresses send.  You'll
never see that happening with the tty interface on Unix (or VMS),
where all nethack ever sees are the ultimate characters being sent by
arbitrary terminals--direct access to the keyboard isn't feasible.

     You have the option of using g/G/5 prefix or switching to the
vi-style movement commands though.  NetHack's input model is based on
characters; once you're outside the port-specific or windowing-specific
I/O code, that's all there is.  And with a telnet connection across the
network, that's basically all there is too.


